        "a": "Wide format: each observation is one row, variables are columns. Example: customer ID, Jan_revenue, Feb_revenue, Mar_revenue. Long format: each observation-variable combo is one row (ID, month, revenue). Wide: good for analysis (one row = one observation), easier stats (correlation between Jan/Feb). Long: good for time-series, repeated measures, databases. Convert wide→long (pivot_longer/melt): when rows represent multiple measurements, need stacking. Example: customer time-series-unpivot months into rows. Convert long→wide (pivot_wider/pivot): when variables are spread across rows, need one observation per row. Example: sales data (customer_id, product, amount)→each product becomes column. Tools: pandas.melt/pivot in Python, tidyr::pivot_longer/wider in R, SQL CASE statements. Real: customer purchase data is long (one row per transaction). Pivot to wide (customer × product matrix) for similarity/clustering analysis. Then pivot back to long for time-series modeling."
