        "a": "Eigenvectors: direction unchanged by matrix multiplication (stays on same line). Eigenvalues: scaling factor. Intuition: squishy ball's principal axes (longest, second-longest directions). Eigenvalues: how much stretches each direction. PCA role: finds max-variance directions in data. Eigenvectors of covariance matrix are those directions. Eigenvalues are variance in each. Why matters: 100 features but only 10 have variance, rest are noise. PCA finds those 10 directions. Drop low-variance (small eigenvalues), keep high-variance (large). Practical: 50 customer features. PCA finds first 5 eigenvectors explain 95% variance (eigenvalues sum to 95%). Drop other 45 (redundant). Train on 5D instead of 50D-10x faster, less overfit. Business: instead of 'which 45 useless?', PCA finds 5 useful directions (eigenvectors). Eigenvalues show information in each. Lose 5% info but gain 10x speed. PCA trades tiny accuracy for big speed/interpretability."
