        "a": "Selection bias: sample isn't representative because selection is non-random. Example 1-Healthcare: hospital patients. Bias: mild cases stay home, severe go to hospital. Sample biased toward severity. Conclusion: disease is severe (wrong). Example 2-Online surveys: email survey. Bias: dissatisfied unsubscribe, don't receive. Sample biased toward satisfied. Result: satisfaction seems high, misses churn risk. Example 3-Hiring: features of hired candidates (college, GPA). Bias: only see hired, not rejected. Sample filtered by decisions. Conclusion: GPA predicts performance (wrong). Detect: compare sample to population. If survey 80% male but population 50%, selection bias. If conclusions change if missing people included, selection bias exists. Example: if dissatisfied sent 'I'm leaving,' would satisfaction drop? Yes→bias. Fix: randomize selection, control for mechanism (weight missing people higher), collect whole population. Selection bias invisible (missing data). Always ask: who isn't in my sample and why?"
