        "a": "Linear regression: fits straight line (y=mx+b). Works: minimize squared errors, closed-form solution. Pros: fast, interpretable (coefficient=y change per x unit), works well for linear relationships. Cons: assumes linearity (fails on curved relationships), sensitive to outliers, struggles with many features. Drawbacks: underfits non-linear (y=x^2, sin). Multicollinearity (correlated features break coefficients). Extrapolation (predicting beyond data range risky). When choose: data is linear (check scatter plots), interpretability critical (board wants to understand), speed matters, few features (<20), enough data. Example: predict revenue from ad spend. If linear relationship, LR is great (fast, explains 'each dollar→X revenue'). If curved (diminishing returns), LR underfits. Modern: start with LR (baseline), compare to complex models. If LR within 5% accuracy, use it (simpler better). Many teams use LR first, add complexity only if needed. Interpretability underrated-stakeholders understand LR, not neural nets."
