        "a": "Correlation vs causation: married status does not cause fewer accidents; confounders drive both. Potential confounding variables: (1) Age-married people older, older drivers more experienced, fewer accidents. (2) Maturity-married people more responsible, fewer risk-taking behaviors. (3) Income-married couples higher income, better vehicle maintenance, safety features. (4) Gender-more women married (in data), women average fewer accidents. (5) Driving purpose-married people commute predictably, fewer risky trips. (6) Selection-responsible people more likely to marry. Proper analysis: (1) Regression: accidents ~ married + age + gender + income + driving_pattern. Is married coefficient still significant after controlling confounders? Likely becomes zero or small. (2) Matching-find married and single drivers identical on age/gender/income/experience, compare accident rates. If no difference, marriage was not causal. (3) A/B test-randomly assign discount to some married drivers, measure accident rates. Unethical but reveals causation. Business risk: if they lower premiums based on spurious correlation, they might cherry-pick safer drivers who happen to be married, losing profitability. Correct approach: control for actual risk factors (age, gender, driving history, location, vehicle type). Marital status adds no predictive power after controlling these. Lesson: always ask-what other variables explain both the predictor and outcome?-before making causal claims."
