        "q": "Batch normalization in deep learning. Speed? When hurt?",
        "tracks": [],
        "subcategory": "machine_learning",
        "difficulty": "Hard",
        "level": "lead_ds",
        "domain": "general",
        "a": "Normalize layer inputs: x_hat=(x-μ)/σ. Scale: y=γ×x_hat+β. Why speed: (1) Stabilizes internal covariate shift, allows larger learning rate. (2) Better gradients, faster convergence. (3) Regularization via batch noise, reduces overfitting. (4) Trains 10-100x faster. When hurt: (1) Small batches-statistics unreliable (batch size 4). (2) Train/test different-training uses batch stats, inference uses running average. Mismatch. (3) RNNs-breaks sequential dependencies. (4) Final layers-before softmax loses scale info. (5) Custom loss-assumes Gaussian input. Best: batch norm after dense layers (before activation). Monitor batch size >16. Running average for inference. Modern: LayerNorm (stable), GroupNorm (batch-size independent)."
